Discuss what these sources say about Genome Quality Filtering, in the larger context of Core Pipeline.
In the core pipelines of advanced computational biology frameworks, genome quality filtering acts as a critical prerequisite step to ensure that downstream analyses, metabolic reconstructions, and machine learning models are built on accurate and reliable data. The sources detail specific quality filtering mechanisms and their broader pipeline contexts primarily within two systems: the Gempipe metabolic modeling tool and the ViroBench viral genomics benchmark.
Genome Quality Filtering in the Gempipe Pipeline Gempipe is a computational tool designed to reconstruct and analyze pan- and multi-strain genome-scale metabolic models (GSMMs). Within its core pipeline, genome quality filtering is fully integrated into the initial phase, commanded by the gempipe recon program. Because missing or fragmented genomic data would result in artificial gaps in the generated metabolic networks, Gempipe automatically evaluates raw genome assemblies before integrating them into its presence/absence matrix (PAM) and drafting a pan-GSMM.
When genomes are inputted into the pipeline, they undergo the following sequential filtering steps:
Gene Annotation and Ortholog Assessment: The pipeline first predicts and annotates genes using Prodigal via Prokka. It then runs BUSCO on the resulting proteomes to quantify the completeness of the genome by identifying the number of missing and fragmented expected single-copy orthologs.
Assembly Metrics: Simultaneously, the tool uses seqkit stats to compute the total number of contigs and the N50 metric (a measure of sequence contiguity) for each genome assembly.
Threshold Enforcement: Strains that fail to meet strict, user-specified quality thresholds are systematically discarded from all subsequent analyses. The default parameters reject any assembly that exhibits more than 2% missing BUSCO orthologs, a 100% fragmented BUSCO rate, an N50 below 50,000, or a total count exceeding 200 contigs.
In the larger context of the Gempipe workflow, this rigorous quality filtering ensures that the subsequent "reference-free" and "reference-based" metabolic network expansions are biologically sound. For example, in a large-scale bioprospecting case study on Limosilactobacillus reuteri, the researchers initially retrieved 1,056 assemblies (including 597 metagenome-assembled genomes). The core pipeline applied taxonomy and quality filtering—which also involved removing contaminating contigs belonging to other species—to narrow the dataset down to 545 high-quality genomes. Only these filtered genomes advanced to the next stages of the pipeline to accurately predict strain-specific traits like reuterin and vitamin B12 synthesis.
Genome Quality Filtering in the ViroBench Pipeline A similarly strict quality filtering approach is utilized in the data curation pipeline of ViroBench, a framework designed to benchmark Nucleotide Foundation Models (NFMs) on viral genomics tasks. Because ViroBench evaluates NFMs on both their biological understanding and their potential latent biosecurity risks across long evolutionary timeframes, the underlying training and testing corpus must be impeccably curated.
The core pipeline for ViroBench relies on a multi-stage filtering process applied to initial sequence records retrieved from the NCBI RefSeq and GenBank databases. To maintain data integrity, the pipeline enforces the following filters:
Entries with incomplete taxonomic hierarchical fields or missing discovery timestamps are immediately removed.
The system strictly retains only verified, high-quality assemblies.
Species-level redundancies are resolved using a hierarchical tie-breaking policy that prioritizes RefSeq reference genomes, assembly completeness (e.g., complete genomes over contigs), and recent updates.
By applying these quality filters, the ViroBench curation pipeline successfully distilled an initial pool of over 273,000 virus-associated sequence IDs down to a highly reliable corpus of 58,314 viral samples. In the larger context of the benchmark, this filtering ensures that the resulting 18 diagnostic scenarios—which test everything from phylogenetic classification to the generation of functional protein-coding sequences—are measuring true model capabilities rather than artifacts caused by poor assembly quality or corrupted metadata.
